title: COWBUTTONS
section: 1
date: @DATE@
source: @VERSION@
volume: cowbuttons - button panel for cow

# NAME

cowbuttons - Button panel for the cow window manager

# SYNOPSIS

**cowbuttons** \[**-c** *config*\] \[**-h**\]

# DESCRIPTION

**cowbuttons** displays a small configurable grid of cells. Cells can run
**cow**(1) commands, periodically display the output of scripts, show selected
state from cow's status socket, or act as empty spacers.

External applications can occupy cells or open beside a panel handle. CoW
attaches their windows to cowbuttons, keeping them together when moved, raised,
or iconified. This uses CoW's container machinery.

# OPTIONS

**-c** *config*

: Load *config* instead of the default configuration file.

**-h**

: Show usage.

# CONFIGURATION

The format is line-oriented. Blank lines and lines starting with **\#** are
ignored. Labels and commands containing whitespace must be quoted.

Colours may be opaque **0xRRGGBB** values or **0xRRGGBBAA** values carrying an
alpha component. In the latter form, **00** is fully transparent and **FF** is
fully opaque. For example, **style button.bg 0x282828CC** uses an 80-percent
opaque dark grey.

**cell** *W* *H*

: Set the default cell size in pixels. The default is 96 by 32.

**grid** *rows* *cols*

: Set the grid size.

**col** *col* **width** *N*

: Set one column's width in pixels.

**fill.width** *true|false*

: A cell property which gives its columns the panel's remaining width.
  For example, add **fill.width true** to an **external** cowiconman cell
  to let it grow while neighbouring buttons retain their configured widths.
  If several cells request it, they share the extra space proportionally.
  Without any fill cells, all non-handle columns grow as before.

**show panel** *name* \[...\]

: Show one or more named panel definitions from the configuration file.
  Multiple names on the same **show panel** line are combined into one
  cowbuttons window. Multiple top-level **show panel** lines create
  separate cowbuttons windows from the same configuration file. Settings
  outside panel blocks can be used as shared defaults.

**panel** *name* \[*direction* \[*property* *value* ...\]\] **{**

: Start a named panel definition. A panel is loaded when it is named by
  **show panel**. When *direction* is **left**, **right**, **up**, or
  **down**, cowbuttons adds an attached handle and opens the panel in
  that direction. Pressing the handle collapses or expands the panel.
  Handle properties use the same styling keys as **button**.
  **handle.size** sets the handle thickness in pixels: its width for
  left/right panels, or its height for up/down panels. The default is 22.
  **handle.both true** adds a second handle at the opposite end. Either
  handle toggles the same panel, keeping the clicked handle in place.
  When collapsed, only the clicked handle remains visible. Reopening
  restores both handles; a panel configured to start closed shows its
  primary handle.
  **handle.opposite.bg** *colour* overrides the second handle's background;
  other styling is shared.
  **parent** *name* hides the panel's handle and applications while the
  named panel is collapsed. Reopening the parent restores the child's
  previous open/closed state. Both panels must be selected in the same
  **show panel** line; parent relationships must not form a cycle.
  **open** *true*\|*false* chooses the initial expanded state. External
  drawers default to closed; other panels default to open.

  Write panel properties on separate lines inside the panel block, before
  its contents. **direction** selects the opening direction. These
  properties affect the panel handle; **style** still sets button defaults.
  The inline header form remains supported.

      panel applications {
          direction right
          handle.size 11
          handle.both true
          handle.opposite.bg 0xcd2626
          frame 2
          bg 0xffd700
          fg 0x202020
          row {
              button "Terminal" "exec foot"
          }
      }

**row** \[**height** *N*\] **{** ... **}**

: Start a new visual row. Cells inside the block are appended
  left-to-right.

**if** \[**not**\] **command** *name* **{** ... **}**

: Process the block only when *name* exists in **PATH** and is
  executable.

**if** \[**not**\] **file** *path* **{** ... **}**

: Process the block only when *path* exists. Relative paths are resolved
  against the configuration file's directory.

**if** \[**not**\] **env** *name* **{** ... **}**

: Process the block only when the environment variable *name* is set and
  non-empty.

**font** *name*

: Set the font face used for labels.

**resizable** *true*\|*false*

: Allow the window to be resized. Content columns and rows grow or shrink
  with the window, while panel handles retain their configured thickness.
  Closing a panel preserves the resized dimensions for reopening.
  The default is *false*.

**close_on_exit** *true*\|*false*

: Set the default for subsequent **external** cells. The default is
  **false**, which releases applications as ordinary windows when
  cowbuttons exits. With **true**, CoW requests that they close instead.
  Each **external** can override this with its own **close_on_exit** value.
  This also applies to reused applications and when cowbuttons is killed.
  It does not forcibly terminate clients: applications may prompt to save
  work or refuse to close. Collapsing a panel never closes its applications.

**style button.bg** *colour*

: Set the normal button background colour.

**style button.bg\_pressed** *colour*

: Set the pressed button background colour.

**style button.fg** *colour*

: Set the normal button text colour.

**style button.fg\_pressed** *colour*

: Set the pressed button text colour.

**style button.frame** *width*

: Set the relief thickness in pixels (default: 2). Positive values give
  a raised frame which becomes sunken when pressed. Negative values reverse
  this; zero draws no frame. Frames are limited to half the cell's smaller
  dimension. Override individual buttons or panel handles with **frame**:

      style button.frame 2
      button "Terminal" "exec foot" frame -2

**style button.image** *path*

: Draw a PNG, SVG, or XPM image in command buttons. Relative paths are
  resolved against the configuration file's directory.

**style button.image\_pressed** *path*

: Draw a PNG, SVG, or XPM image while a command button is pressed.

**style button.image\_size** *N*

: Set the image size in pixels. The default is 16.

**style button.image\_position** *left*\|*center*\|*right*\|*fill*

: Set where button images are drawn. The default is *left*.

**style button.justify** *left*\|*center*\|*right*

: Set the default button text justification. The default is *left*.

**button** *label* *command* \[*property* *value* ...\]

: Create a clickable button. *command* is sent to cow as its left-click
  action. Supported properties are **width**, **height**, **span**,
  **frame**, **bg**, **bg\_pressed**, **fg**, **fg\_pressed**, **image**,
  **image\_pressed**, **image\_size**, **image\_position**, **justify**,
  and the click-action properties described below.

**leftclick** *command* **click** *command* **middleclick** *command* **rightclick** *command*

: Set the single-click action for the specific button. **click** is an
  alias for **leftclick**.

**doubleclick** *command* **leftdoubleclick** *command* **middledoubleclick** *command* **rightdoubleclick** *command*

: Set a double-click action. **doubleclick** is an alias for
  **leftdoubleclick**. When both single- and double-click actions exist
  for the same button, the single-click action is delayed for 300 ms.

**script** *command* **interval** *seconds* \[*property* *value* ...\]

: Create a cell whose label is supplied by an external command. The
  command is run through the shell immediately after startup and then
  again the configured number of seconds after each run finishes. Runs
  never overlap.

  The first line written to standard output, limited to 255 bytes,
  replaces the cell's label when the command exits successfully. Empty
  output clears the label. If the command fails, the previous successful
  label is retained and an error is written to standard error.

  Script cells support the same click actions, styling, and geometry
  properties as **button** cells.

**external** *command* \[*property* *value* ...\]

: Create an application slot. In a directional panel, the application
  starts on first opening and appears beside the handle. Collapsing the
  panel hides its window without terminating it; reopening shows the
  same window. Directional external panels start collapsed.

  Inside a **row**, each **external** occupies its own cell alongside
  buttons, spacers or other applications. Applications start when the
  panel is open. Collapsing its handle hides every attached application;
  reopening restores the same windows. Without a direction the row stays
  open. A standalone **external** outside a row retains the directional
  drawer behaviour described above; only one such drawer is allowed per
  panel.
  Upward and downward drawers follow the width of their grid span while
  retaining their configured height. Use **span** to extend the handle
  and application across neighbouring columns.
  Applications are inset inside their slot's **frame**, including external
  drawers. The frame is drawn by cowbuttons, not by a window decoration
  rule. **padding** *pixels* adds space between the frame and application,
  using the cell's background colour. It defaults to zero. Set **frame 0**
  and **padding 0** on an external cell to use the whole slot.
  An external cell inside a row may specify **handle.size** *pixels* to
  add a right-edge toggle. This hides only that application; reopening
  reuses the same window. **collapse retain** (the default) keeps the cell's
  size, background and relief visible without moving neighbouring buttons.
  **collapse shrink** reduces its column to the handle width instead.
  Such cells must occupy one column, but may span several rows.
  If an embedded application closes or fails to start, click its vacant
  cell to launch it again. Applications which enforce a minimum size or
  aspect ratio may leave unused space or be clipped to the cell.

  CoW manages the attachment automatically. No container definitions or
  placement rules are required. Closing cowbuttons releases attached
  applications as ordinary windows unless **close_on_exit true** is set.
  Attachments survive a CoW restart while cowbuttons remains running.

  Attached applications cannot be moved, resized or dragged out independently.
  Move the cowbuttons host to move the complete panel.

  The application ID defaults to the command's executable name, matched
  case-insensitively. Use **app_id** *name* when a wrapper or application
  uses a different ID. **title** *pattern* optionally narrows matching using
  a case-sensitive shell wildcard pattern. By default only newly opened
  windows are candidates. With **use_old true**, cowbuttons first looks for
  an existing matching window and launches the command only if none exists.
  Existing windows are captured even when the panel starts closed, and
  remain hidden until it opens. Missing applications are launched only
  when the panel opens.
  Windows already in another attachment or container are never captured.
  If several existing windows match, the cell remains vacant and logs a
  warning; narrow the match before retrying. With **close_on_exit false**,
  closing the host releases clients for another instance to reuse.

  Newly launched commands must open a unique matching window within ten
  seconds. Use distinct panel names for separate cowbuttons instances so
  their host windows can be identified.

  Supported properties are **width**, **height**, **span**, **bg**,
  **bg\_pressed**, **fg**, **fg\_pressed**, **image**, **image\_pressed**,
  **image\_size**, **image\_position**, **justify**, **app_id**, **title**,
  **use_old** (default **false**), and **close_on_exit**.

  **respawn true** reruns the command when its attached window closes,
  including a manual close. The default is **false**. Cowbuttons waits at
  least one second before relaunching and defers this while the panel is
  collapsed. The launcher process exiting does not trigger respawn if its
  window remains open. Exiting cowbuttons never respawns clients.

  If a launch fails to produce a matching window, automatic retries stop;
  click the vacant cell or reopen the panel to try again. This also prevents
  a broken command from repeatedly launching processes.

  For example, a horizontal panel with its handle on the left:

      show panel bar
      panel bar {
          direction right
          row {
              external "cowpager -1" width 160 height 96
              button "Windows" "window-list" width 80 height 96
              external "foot" width 480 height 96
              external "cowiconman" width 300 height 96
          }
      }

  To close the pager and icon manager on exit while keeping the terminal:

      close_on_exit true
      show panel bar
      panel bar {
          direction right
          row {
              external "cowpager -1" width 160 height 96
              external "foot" close_on_exit false width 480 height 96
              external "cowiconman" width 300 height 96
          }
      }

**status** *type* \[*property* *value* ...\]

: Create a live status cell. Supported types are: **focused-title**,
  **focused-app**, **current-output**, **current-desk**, and
  **desk-list**.

**spacer** \[*property* *value* ...\]

: Create an empty cell.

# EXAMPLES

    show panel launcher
    show panel telephone
    show panel pager
    show panel statusbar

    resizable true

    style button.bg 0x39414a
    style button.bg_pressed 0x242a30
    style button.fg 0x1f2933
    style button.fg_pressed 0xffffff
    style button.image_size 18

    panel launcher {
        cell 118 30
        grid 2 4

        row height 30 {
            button "Terminal" "exec xterm" bg 0xcde7d8 image cowbuttons/svg/terminal.svg
            button "Firefox" "exec firefox" bg 0xffddc8 image cowbuttons/svg/browser.svg
            button "Files" "exec thunar" bg 0xffe4ad image cowbuttons/svg/files.svg
            button "Editor" "exec emacs" bg 0xded7f5 image cowbuttons/svg/editor.svg
        }

        row height 30 {
            button "Next" "focus -n" bg 0xf6e2a6
            button "Prev" "focus -p" bg 0xcfe8db
        }
    }

    panel telephone {
        direction left
        bg 0xf2d6a6
        cell 42 28
        grid 5 3

        row height 22 {
            button "Telephone" "nop" span fill bg 0xe8d9f2
        }
    }

    panel pager {
        direction right
        bg 0x3f5b51
        external "cowpager" width 420 height 120 bg 0x242a30
    }

    panel statusbar {
        cell 140 30
        grid 1 2

        row {
            script "~/.local/bin/clock.sh" interval 1 click "exec gsimplecal" justify center
            script "~/.local/bin/battery.sh" interval 30 justify center
        }
    }

Remove cowbuttons' own window decors using a cow rule:

    decor -d cowbuttons-nodecor border.width 0
    decor -d cowbuttons-nodecor border.handles false
    decor -d cowbuttons-nodecor titlebar.enabled false
    rule -g -Ton-map -ncowbuttons-chrome -s %cowbuttons decor -a cowbuttons-nodecor

# FILES

*\$XDG\_CONFIG\_HOME/cow/cowbuttons.conf*

*~/.config/cow/cowbuttons.conf*

# SEE ALSO

**cow**(1), **moocow**(1), **cowbar**(1), **cowiconman**(1), **cowpager**(1)

